  "sec": 13.6,
  "recommendation_rationale": "For a two-stage system that RE-RANKS the candidate set, candidate FIDELITY (overlap@K) matters, not just whether the gold survives. FlatIP exact = lossless (overlap 1.0) at p95 0.386ms (~47% faster than numpy brute 0.723ms) -> safe default. HNSW ef64 = near-lossless candidate fidelity (overlap@20 0.992, rec_Recall@20 degradation 0.0) at p95 0.160ms (~2.4x faster than FlatIP, ~4.5x faster than brute) -> approximate option when latency/scale demands it. Aggressive IVF (nprobe<=16) preserves downstream rec_Recall@20 (gold survives) but candidate overlap collapses to 0.28-0.78 -> risky for a re-ranking pipeline; not recommended.",
  "current_scale_caveat": "At 40,541 items all settings are sub-millisecond; ANN approximation is primarily a SCALE story (10-100x catalog). Latency wins are real and measured but absolute latencies are tiny here.",
  "proven_stronger_claim": "FAISS HNSW ef64 preserved candidate retrieval quality within measured tolerance (overlap@20 0.992, rec-Recall@20 degradation 0.0) while reducing p95 latency ~2.4x vs exact FlatIP and ~4.5x vs numpy brute-force (CPU in-sandbox). FlatIP exact reduces p95 ~47% vs brute at zero quality loss."
